course: dictionary
lesson: 12497
title: Teeth and their dentition
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Dentition is a big word for something you can see every time you smile: the set of teeth in a mouth. It tells you how many teeth there are, what kinds they are and how they are arranged.
explain: Dentition can also mean the growing of teeth. «The baby's dentition is starting, and a new tooth has just come through.» Here it means the teeth are coming in, which often happens in babies and young children.
explain: Children have milk dentition first, which means baby teeth, and later permanent dentition, which means the teeth that stay. Read the words around dentition to find the clue, like the word milk or permanent.
fact: Dentition means the set of teeth in a mouth, including how many there are, what kinds they are and how they are arranged.
fact: Dentition can also mean the growing of teeth, especially in babies and children.
fact: «Milk dentition» means baby teeth, and «permanent dentition» means the teeth that stay.
fact: Scientists study an animal's dentition to find out what it eats.
fact: Sharp canines are for eating meat, and flat molars are for eating plants.
exercise: «The dentist looked carefully at Sam's dentition.» What does dentition mean here? | The sore feeling after a fall | A type of toothbrush | *The set of teeth in his mouth and how they are arranged | A dentist's bill
exercise: «The wolf has sharp canines in its dentition.» What do sharp canines suggest the wolf eats? | Grass | Sand | Only fruit | *Meat
exercise: What does «milk dentition» mean? | Teeth that have fallen out | Teeth made from milk | *Baby teeth | Teeth that stay for life
test: «The baby's dentition is growing fast.» What does dentition mean here? | Gum disease | A kind of toothbrush | A set of plates for a dog | *The growing of teeth
test: «The rabbit has flat molars in its dentition, good for grinding plants.» What does this show about the rabbit? | *It eats plants | It eats meat | It can fly | It has no teeth
exam: Which phrase means the teeth that last into adulthood? | *permanent dentition | famished dentition | milk dentition | barely dentition
